{
 "turns_text": [
  "[SEG0001] person_1 (NURSE): Hey there, I'll grab vitals real quick before she comes in. Jacket can stay on.",
  "[SEG0002] person_2 (PATIENT): Sure. This whole thing is, uh, kind of pointless.",
  "[SEG0003] person_3 (FAMILY_MEMBER): Don't start. I dragged him here. He's been miserable since the trees bloomed and he keeps saying he's fine.",
  "[SEG0004] person_1 (NURSE): Blood pressure 122 over 76, pulse 68, temp 36.8 celsius, oxygen 99 percent, respirations 16. Alright, you're set.",
  "[SEG0005] person_4 (DOCTOR): Hi, I'm Dr. Shah. What brings you in?",
  "[SEG0006] person_2 (PATIENT): Err, just the spring sneezing. It's nothing. She panics every year.",
  "[SEG0007] person_4 (DOCTOR): Spring sneezing. Is the nose running too, or is it mostly sneezes?",
  "[SEG0008] person_2 (PATIENT): It runs, yeah. Clear stuff. I'm not, like, dying.",
  "[SEG0009] person_4 (DOCTOR): Eyes?",
  "[SEG0010] person_2 (PATIENT): They itch a little. Both of them. Barely, though.",
  "[SEG0011] person_3 (FAMILY_MEMBER): Barely. They're red. He sits there rubbing them all through dinner.",
  "[SEG0012] person_2 (PATIENT): I rub them sometimes. People have eyes.",
  "[SEG0013] person_4 (DOCTOR): How long has this round been going?",
  "[SEG0014] person_2 (PATIENT): About two weeks. Same thing every spring.",
  "[SEG0015] person_4 (DOCTOR): Any fever?",
  "[SEG0016] person_2 (PATIENT): No. I don't do fevers.",
  "[SEG0017] person_4 (DOCTOR): And congestion, like a stuffy nose?",
  "[SEG0018] person_2 (PATIENT): No, not really. It just runs.",
  "[SEG0019] person_4 (DOCTOR): Mm. I'm sorting through a spring cold, hay fever, maybe the lungs. I'm not gonna decide it's a sinus infection off this.",
  "[SEG0020] person_3 (FAMILY_MEMBER): It's every April, doctor. And, um, I'm his sister. I'm allergic to dust, badly. We share an apartment so I notice everything.",
  "[SEG0021] person_2 (PATIENT): She mentions the dust like it's a hobby. I just live there.",
  "[SEG0022] person_4 (DOCTOR): Your chart says asthma. Still dealing with that?",
  "[SEG0023] person_2 (PATIENT): Yeah, I guess. Since college. Super mild. I forget it's even there.",
  "[SEG0024] person_4 (DOCTOR): What do you take for the asthma?",
  "[SEG0025] person_2 (PATIENT): Albuterol. Two puffs if I need it, which is like a few times a year. Nothing.",
  "[SEG0026] person_4 (DOCTOR): Anything for the sneezing?",
  "[SEG0027] person_2 (PATIENT): Umm, ceterizine. Zyrtec. Ten milligrams, sometimes, when it bugs me.",
  "[SEG0028] person_3 (FAMILY_MEMBER): Some mornings he remembers. Most mornings the bottle just sits there.",
  "[SEG0029] person_4 (DOCTOR): Any wheezing with this episode?",
  "[SEG0030] person_2 (PATIENT): No. I never wheeze when it's the spring thing.",
  "[SEG0031] person_4 (DOCTOR): What about shortness of breath?",
  "[SEG0032] person_2 (PATIENT): No... well, sometimes. If I chase the dog around the block. It's not a thing.",
  "[SEG0033] person_4 (DOCTOR): Chest pain?",
  "[SEG0034] person_2 (PATIENT): Not that either.",
  "[SEG0035] person_4 (DOCTOR): Let me play that back. Couple weeks of sneezing, runny nose, itchy eyes, like every spring. Asthma since college, albuterol now and then, ceterizine ten milligrams sometimes. No wheeze, no chest pain. Short of breath only sometimes when you chase the dog.",
  "[SEG0036] person_2 (PATIENT): Yeah. That's it. Can we be done, the parking meter is, uh, not generous.",
  "[SEG0037] person_4 (DOCTOR): Almost. How's the rest of you, stomach okay, any nausea or change in your bowels?",
  "[SEG0038] person_2 (PATIENT): Nope. Gut's totally fine.",
  "[SEG0039] person_3 (FAMILY_MEMBER): His nose plugs up every single night. He mouth breathes. He told you it isn't stuffy and that is just not true.",
  "[SEG0040] person_2 (PATIENT): Okay, fine. It does get stuffed up at night. I didn't think that counted. It's not bad.",
  "[SEG0041] person_4 (DOCTOR): Right. Stuffed up at night, I'll put that down. Let me look.",
  "[SEG0042] person_4 (DOCTOR): Eyes are a bit watery on both sides, no pus. The lining in the nose is pale and boggy. Lungs are clear, I don't hear a wheeze.",
  "[SEG0043] person_4 (DOCTOR): My read is seasonal allergic rhinitis. I'm not calling this an asthma flare, and I'm not ready to call it an infection either.",
  "[SEG0044] person_2 (PATIENT): So allergies. I knew that. This was a whole trip for allergies.",
  "[SEG0045] person_4 (DOCTOR): I'd like you to stop the ceterizine. We'll switch to fexofenadine, 180 milligrams, one pill by mouth every morning, through the season.",
  "[SEG0046] person_4 (DOCTOR): And eye drops, olopatadine zero point one percent, one drop in each eye twice a day.",
  "[SEG0047] person_2 (PATIENT): Drops. Great. I'll, um, try to remember those too.",
  "[SEG0048] person_4 (DOCTOR): Don't rub the eyes, it feeds the itch. Keep the windows shut in the morning when the pollen's high, and do a saline rinse in the nose at night.",
  "[SEG0049] person_4 (DOCTOR): If it has not settled in three weeks, come back and see me. If you start wheezing or you can't catch your breath, don't wait, call us sooner."
 ],
